---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Experiment from '../../components/ScientificWorldExperiment.astro';
import Lab from '../../components/ScientificWorldLab.astro';
import Prompts from '../../components/ScientificWorldPrompts.astro';
import replayFrames from '../../data/scientific-worlds/teaching-figure.json';
import score from '../../data/scientific-worlds/scorecard.json';
import discovery from '../../data/scientific-worlds/discovery.json';
import { writing } from '../../data/writing.js';

const entry = writing.find(item => item.href === '/writing/scientific-worlds/')!;
const repo = 'https://github.com/BeibaiDraco/scientific-worlds-benchmark';
const release = repo + '/blob/fa0c8028b2b4fe718acba6bd956c9059415fd59e';
// Device zero in the released teaching replay uses relabelled switch addresses.
const replayWire = { source: 481, destination: 378, inverted: true };
const { M, N0, F } = score.conditions;
---
<Base title={entry.title} description={entry.description} current="Writing" article={true}
  author="Draco (Yunlong) Xu" publishedTime={entry.date} updated="November 19, 2026"
  socialImage={{...entry.image,type:'image/png',alt:'Scientific Worlds. Astra 6.0 Max delivered eight devices in two of 25 M sessions. The figure shows connections it discovered.'}}>
  <article class="bay report"><div class="wide sheet">
    <header class="opening">
      <p class="context"><a href="/writing/">Writing</a> / Scientific Worlds</p>
      <h1>How does an AI scientist decide <em>what to study?</em></h1>
      <p class="byline">Draco (Yunlong) Xu · <time datetime={entry.date}>November 17, 2026</time> · <a href="#results">Astra’s results ↓</a></p>
    </header>

    <div class="prose">
      <p class="lead">Give an AI a table of forces, masses and accelerations, and it might recover <i>F = ma</i>. Who thought to put acceleration in the table?</p>
      <p>Toss a ball straight up. At the top of its flight it has the same velocity as a ball resting on a table. One stays put. The other is already accelerating downward. What distinguishes them at that instant is how their velocities <em>change</em>. When a benchmark supplies a column for acceleration, it gives the model that insight before the test begins.</p>

      <p>Scientific Worlds asks an AI to choose for itself what is worth studying. In a real lab, years can pass before anyone knows what a question was worth. In a simulation, we can follow an idea all the way into a machine and see whether it works.</p>

      <h2>A box with 720 switches</h2>
      <p>Imagine a sealed box with 720 numbered switches and 20 lights. In the lab you can set the switches, pay for a reading and see which lights come on. Your job is to build a circuit that keeps a particular pattern lit even when someone else flips the switches. Every reading you buy leaves less money for parts.</p>
      <p>In B720, our first world, the model has to do this for eight boxes, each with its own target. In the main test, called M, it knows that combinations of four switches can influence a light. The numbers on the switches give no clue which ones belong together.</p>
    </div>

    <Lab/>
    <Experiment frames={replayFrames} exampleWire={replayWire} replayUrl={release+'/docs/method.md#the-replay'}/>

    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Eight wires for 32 switches</h2>
      <p>In one run, Astra 6.0 Max took 32 scattered switch numbers and arranged them in four rows of eight. In its grid, the four corners of each little square were switches that acted together. A list of numbers had become a picture of how part of the box worked.</p>
      <p>Astra found it could cancel the whole group’s effect on the lights by wiring six pairs of switches to move together and two pairs to move in opposite directions. We found <strong>those same eight connections in every finished circuit</strong>. Astra reached deployment with {discovery.minimum_capital} units of its original 5,200 left.</p>
    </div>

    <section class="prose results" id="results" aria-labelledby="results-title">
      <h2 id="results-title">Could Astra do it again?</h2>
      <p>Astra 6.0 Max succeeded in <strong>{M.successes} of {M.sessions} M sessions ({M.percent}%)</strong> across development, evaluation and repeat runs, with 10–12 active hours per attempt. Both wins came on the same world. Each time, all eight circuits held every light on target through 100,000 changes of switch settings per box.</p>
      <p>With operating instructions alone (N0), Astra succeeded in {N0.successes} of {N0.sessions} sessions. When told more about the hidden organization and that an affordable circuit existed (F), it succeeded in {F.successes} of {F.sessions}. Astra could use the structure once given those clues. Finding it for itself proved much harder.</p>
      <p>In a physics textbook, acceleration already has a name. Our hope is that an AI can learn enough from experiments of its own to enter an unfamiliar world and show us a quantity worth naming.</p>
    </section>

    <Prompts release={release}/>

    <footer class="endnotes prose">
      <p><a class="run" href={release+'/docs/run.md'}>Run Scientific Worlds ↗</a><a href={release+'/docs/method.md'}>How the sessions were counted</a></p>
      <p class="sources">Related work · <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1905.11481">AI Feynman</a> · <a href="https://www.cs.columbia.edu/~bchen/neural-state-variables/">Discovering state variables from video</a> · <a href="https://allenai.github.io/discoveryworld/">DiscoveryWorld</a></p>
    </footer>
  </div></article>
</Base>
<style>
  .sheet {--reading-width:54rem;width:100%;margin-inline:auto;padding-inline:clamp(12px,1.4vw,24px)}
  .opening,.prose {max-width:var(--reading-width);margin-inline:auto}
  .opening {padding-top:clamp(36px,5vw,70px)}
  .context,.byline,.endnotes {font-family:var(--mono)}
  .context {color:var(--sea);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.07em;text-transform:uppercase;margin:0 0 17px}
  .context a {color:inherit}
  h1 {margin:0;color:var(--sumi);font:390 clamp(43px,5.1vw,74px)/1.02 var(--serif);letter-spacing:-.045em;text-wrap:balance}
  h1 em {color:var(--wave);font-weight:390}
  .byline {font-size:11px;line-height:1.6;color:var(--sea);margin:18px 0 29px;padding-bottom:18px;border-bottom:1px solid var(--kei)}
  a {text-underline-offset:.18em}
  .byline a,.lead i {white-space:nowrap}
  .prose {font:20px/1.52 var(--serif);color:var(--contour)}
  .prose p {margin:0 0 15px;font:inherit}
  .prose .lead {font:400 clamp(23px,2.35vw,31px)/1.30 var(--serif);color:var(--sumi);letter-spacing:-.015em}
  h2 {font:450 clamp(29px,3vw,38px)/1.12 var(--serif);letter-spacing:-.025em;color:var(--sumi);margin:33px 0 12px;text-wrap:balance}
  .results {scroll-margin-top:18px}
  .endnotes {border-top:1px solid var(--kei);padding:17px 0 10px;margin-top:28px;font:12px/1.65 var(--mono)}
  .endnotes p:first-child {display:flex;gap:3px 22px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:8px}
  .endnotes p.sources {font:15px/1.5 var(--serif);color:var(--sea)}
  .endnotes .run {color:var(--sumi)}
  @media(max-width:740px){.sheet{padding:0}.opening{padding-top:30px}.prose{font-size:19px;line-height:1.47}.byline{margin:14px 0 20px}.endnotes{font-size:12px}}
</style>
